# Exact process-identity translations used by exitlag-netd --identity-map.
# Native format: SOURCE<TAB>WINDOWS_PATH (literal tabs).
# Prefix format: wineprefix:DIR<TAB>WINDOWS_PATH<TAB>OWNER_EXE.
#
# SOURCE may be one of:
#   - the canonical absolute path from /proc/<pid>/exe, for a NATIVE Linux game;
#   - wineprefix:<absolute prefix dir>, requiring a third OWNER_EXE column.
#     The actual /proc/PID/exe must equal that canonical approved executable,
#     and its canonical WINEPREFIX must match DIR. Do not guess OWNER_EXE from
#     argv, comm, or a runtime name. Observe the actual socket owner.
#     Multiple owner executables may be approved in separate rows for a prefix.
#     Scope covers everything an approved runtime launches in that prefix;
#     this is a same-user allowlist, not proof of a particular Windows image.
# Legacy prefix-only rows are rejected; add the verified OWNER_EXE column.
# argv tokens are never identity proof. Legacy argv-only entries must be
# replaced by the canonical executable actually owning the socket.
#
# Examples are commented out because nonexistent absolute sources intentionally
# fail startup. Copy this file and uncomment/edit only paths installed locally.
# native Linux game (source = /proc/<pid>/exe)
# /opt/my-native-game/bin/game	C:\Games\Supported Game\game.exe
# another Wine prefix
# wineprefix:/home/USER/prefixes/mygame	C:\Games\Supported Game\Game.exe	/absolute/verified/runtime
# Proton (one prefix per app id)
# wineprefix:/home/USER/.local/share/Steam/steamapps/compatdata/12345/pfx	C:\Games\Supported Game\Game.exe	/absolute/verified/runtime
